<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Learned About the History of Panama. – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning to Research History</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Learned About the History of Panama.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze informational content for specific data. Reading: Summarize narratives of significant events, recent or historical.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify key details and perspectives in a text about Panama's history (e.g., "What year did Panama declare independence, and how is this event described in different texts?"). Can compare the main events of Panama's history as presented in two different narratives (e.g., "How does this source describe the canal's construction compared to a second source?"). Can evaluate a historical timeline by explaining the significance of specific events in context (e.g., "Why was the completion of the Panama Canal in 1914 a pivotal event in global trade?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a world map and points to Panama. Students are asked to brainstorm words related to "Panama" and "history" (e.g., canal, independence, culture, transformation, legacy). The teacher writes these on the board, encouraging students to recall vocabulary from the previous lesson. Then, the teacher shows two contrasting images: one of the construction of the Panama Canal (e.g., workers digging) and another of a modern cargo ship passing through the canal today. Students discuss briefly what they see and what they already know about these images, activating prior knowledge about Panama's rich historical events.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words from the brainstorming activity, emphasizing difficult sounds and stress patterns. For example, for "research," the teacher explicitly shows the noun stress on the first syllable and the verb stress on the second syllable. The teacher also models the pronunciation of -ed endings in words like "declared" /d/ and "completed" /ɪd/, and contrasts sounds like /s/ in "significance" and /z/ in "was." The teacher states, "Today, we will read about significant events that shaped Panama's identity." and models reading a short sentence related to the images, highlighting intonation for historical facts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'research' a noun or a verb when the stress is on the first syllable?" (Expected answer: Noun)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'declared,' what sound do you hear at the end?" (Expected answer: /d/ sound)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning to Research History."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short historical report (article excerpt, B1.1 level) titled "Key Milestones in Panama's History: Independence and the Canal" and a separate, shorter paragraph offering a slightly different perspective on the canal's legacy. The texts will include grammar points like past simple (Panama declared independence), passive voice (The canal was completed), and reported speech (Historians say the canal transformed Panama's economy). Vocabulary words such as <i>independence, separation, canal, construction, economy, significance, transformation, legacy, heritage, declared, completed, shaped, analyze, understand, explain</i> will be systematically embedded.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Analyze informational content for specific data.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the main article quickly to identify the two most significant historical events mentioned. (e.g., "What are the two major historical events discussed in the text?")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read a series of statements and determine if they are true or false based on the main article. (e.g., "Panama declared independence in 1904. True or False?")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match specific years (e.g., 1903, 1914, 1999) to the corresponding historical events described in the text (e.g., "Panama's separation from Colombia," "Completion of the Canal," "Transfer of the Canal to Panama").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying and extracting specific details, understanding the sequence of events using past simple, and recognizing passive voice constructions. Focus on vocabulary such as <i>significance, construction, economy, revolution, transformation, heritage, analyze, summarize, explain</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Gap-Fill & Timeline Completion:</b> Students reread the main article and complete sentences using target vocabulary (e.g., "Panama ______ independence in 1903.") or fill in missing dates/events on a skeletal timeline provided by the teacher. They must identify the verbs in past simple or passive voice. (e.g., "The Panama Canal ______ in 1914, and its ______ transformed global trade.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Summarizing Key Details:</b> In pairs, students identify and underline the sentences that describe the main cause and main effect of Panama's independence and the canal's construction. They then use 2-3 sentences to summarize each event in their own words, focusing on historical accuracy and using vocabulary like <i>shaped, significance, transformation</i>. (e.g., "Panama's separation from Colombia in 1903 was a turning point because it led to the canal's construction, which boosted global trade.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Comparing Perspectives & Clarification:</b> Students read the second, shorter paragraph, which presents a slightly different perspective on the canal's social or environmental impact. In pairs, they discuss: "How does this second source describe the canal's legacy compared to the first source?" They practice asking for clarification on historical facts: "Do you think the construction of the canal helped Panama's economy immediately?" or "Why do you believe independence shaped Panama's national identity?" They should use reported speech when sharing what the texts "say" or "report."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning to Research History."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Historical Summary and Evaluation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group is given a large piece of paper or access to a digital collaboration tool. Their task is to create a "Historical Snapshot" of Panama, summarizing the main events (independence, canal construction/transfer) from the provided texts and evaluating their significance. They must:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Summarize:</b> Write a concise summary (3-4 sentences) for each major event, identifying key details and perspectives from the texts. They should use past simple and passive voice where appropriate.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Compare:</b> Briefly explain how the two texts presented similar or different perspectives on the canal's impact or legacy. They can use reported speech (e.g., "Source A reported that... while Source B explained...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Evaluate Significance:</b> Add a statement evaluating the historical significance of one chosen event, explaining "Why was the completion of the Panama Canal in 1914 a pivotal event in global trade?" using adjectives like <i>relevant, significant, complex, revolutionary</i>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>4. Share:</b> Groups briefly present their "Historical Snapshot" to another group, engaging in short discussions about their findings and opinions. (e.g., "Panama's separation from Colombia in 1903 was a turning point because it led to the canal's construction, which boosted global trade.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and a quick review of "Historical Snapshots."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' written "Historical Snapshot" and their participation in group discussions/presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify key details and perspectives from the texts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate summarization of significant events.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective comparison of information from two sources.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (<i>independence, canal, significance, transformation, legacy</i>) and grammatical structures (past simple, passive voice, reported speech).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation regarding historical significance.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and engagement in group discussion.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new historical fact about Panama I learned today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/challenging to (analyze specific data / compare narratives / explain significance) because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did reading different historical accounts help me understand the complexity of Panama's past?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategy (e.g., identifying keywords, summarizing, comparing) did I find most useful today, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The group activity for comparing perspectives worked well, allowing students to engage with the texts critically and apply the target language. Students demonstrated a good grasp of the past simple and could summarize key events. Some students found it challenging to articulate the "significance" of events in context, requiring more scaffolding for analytical vocabulary. For the next lesson, I will provide sentence starters for expressing opinions and significance, and dedicate more time to practicing reported speech in spoken interactions. The pronunciation practice for -ed endings and 'research' stress will be revisited in future warm-ups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the historical facts and analyses learned in this reading lesson to engage in structured debates and discussions about the impact and legacy of these events on Panama's national identity and culture, practicing asking about important historical events and sharing opinions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>